INT. ART RESTORATION LAB - NIGHT

White light over an easel. Solvent fumes. A large Dutch still life lies on the table, its lower third scraped to the ground layer.

CELESTE DUMAS, 40s, conservator, in white gloves and a magnifying visor, works a cotton swab along a patch of ochre with the calm of someone defusing a bomb. IVO, 30s, her assistant, photographs each stroke. SABINE, 20s, a student, holds a lamp so steady it seems to be part of her arm.

CELESTE
There's something under the lemon.

IVO
Under?

SABINE
It's a signature.

CELESTE
It's not the artist's.

IVO
Whose?

CELESTE
Look at the letters. They're pressed in from behind.

The swab pauses. Under the lemon, a ghostly mark appears, a thin cross-hatched initial. Ivo steps closer, his camera dropping.

IVO
That's the Fenwick stamp.

SABINE
The forgery ring?

CELESTE
Not a forgery. A transfer. The original is under this one.

The door at the end of the lab clicks open. All three look up. ANTON KOLOSOV, 50s, in a long coat dusted with rain, stands in the doorway holding a key card in one hand and a thin black briefcase in the other.

KOLOSOV
Don't touch the lemon.

CELESTE
Mr. Kolosov. How did you get in?

KOLOSOV
I paid for the lock.

IVO
You were told the lab is closed.

KOLOSOV
And I was told this painting was clean. We are all disappointed tonight.

Sabine lowers the lamp. Light slides off the canvas and across the floor, across his shoes. Celeste straightens, and her hand, quietly, finds the solvent bottle.
